// probe: pragma_only
//
// The control that everything above was missing.
//
// Every probe in this directory carries the same line - #pragma plugin nvmidi
// - and none of them ever tested it. c_handle.nvgt differs from k_lit.nvgt by
// exactly one line: c declares midi_output@ out = null, k declares int k = 1.
// Both carry the pragma. k passed with exit 0 and no output; c failed at its
// own declaration line. So the pragma is not the fault and the plugin loads.
//
// What that leaves is the reading the whole session was one step away from:
// the engine refuses the '@' spelling only when the base name is not a type it
// knows. midi_note@ and midi_config@ are accepted two lines above midi_input@
// in n_type.nvgt under the same spelling, so '@' cannot be both legal and
// illegal in one translation unit. The spelling is not the defect; the missing
// registration is.
//
// This file asks that question with nothing else in it. It does not declare a
// port and it does not declare a note - if the plugin's registration were
// intact, the pragma alone would still be a legal script.
void main() {
	print("PRAGMASTART");
	print("PRAGMAEND");
}
